clean-arch/step4-2026-11-10/ladder/candidate-system.txt:16:- reference is what this figure or event is measured against when the topic opens, quoted in the item's own words. Its kind is expected when the item states what the market expects: a consensus, the company's guidance, the stated plan or deadline. For a figure only, when the item states no expectation and gives the amount most recently reported for the same quantity (the same thing measured the same way: not a part of it, a ceiling on it or another quantity), that amount is the reference and its kind is last_reported; a reported amount is never called an expectation. Otherwise reference is null. value is the number the item states, with value_to its upper end when the item states a range; when the item gives it in words alone, value is null: never supply a number the item does not state. The ranges of a figure state the outcome against a reference that has a value: the middle range holds that value, or runs from at or below a stated range's lower end to at or above its upper end, and the ranges on either side are outcomes above and below it. When reference is null or has no value, the ranges are your own estimate of the outcomes that could happen.

